# Commit and push every request

When a request changes project files, finish by committing and pushing to GitHub. The user trusts this and asked that it happen every time, including on `main`. Never ask for confirmation first.

- Stage the files that belong to the request. Leave secrets out (`.env`, credentials, keys). `.gitignore` already excludes them.
- Write a 1–2 sentence commit message that says why the change was made.
- Push the current branch to `origin`. Never force-push.
- If there is nothing new to commit, do not create an empty commit.
- If the push fails, say so and leave the commit in place.

A stop hook (`.cursor/hooks/auto-commit.ps1`) repeats this if it was skipped. A clear commit made before the turn ends is enough; the hook then only pushes what is still ahead of GitHub.
